// loft#1647 — a record built by a call and MOVED into a container is released once.
// `m.vs += [vert(a, up)]`, `h.q = vert(c, up)` and `m.vs[1] = vert(c, up)` copy the call's
// result with the `0x8000` move bit, which releases the call's store inside the copy; the
// scope pass lifts the call into a `__lift_N` temp and used to release that store AGAIN at
// the temp's scope exit.  Silent while the slot stayed free — and the lazily minted return
// buffer (`@FR-O-LazyBuffer`) put an allocation in that window, so the second release freed
// a live buffer and the next pass read whatever took its slot (M1: element 6 read pass 1's
// `a`).  The move bit is set only for a call that answers a store of its own: a callee that
// can hand back its ARGUMENT's record (M6–M8, `same(w)`) keeps its private copy and one
// release, and `w` survives.  Every value is hand-computed; the two backends must agree.
// `LOFT_POISON=1`, `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1` and `LOFT_NATIVE_LEAK_CHECK=1` must answer
// identically.
// @falsified-at: 587784455 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1647-a-moved-call-result-is-released-once.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
//   CHANNEL: the VALUE channel, on both backends alike — the control releases a lifted call
//   result a second time, and the store it frees is by then another local's buffer.
//   WITNESS: on 587784455 M1 prints `0,0 0,0 10,1 10,1 1,1 1,1 0,0 11,2` where this file says
//   `… 11,2 11,2` (element 6 is pass 1's `a`), M3 and M4 read `0,0` in their second slot, M9
//   repeats M1 through a parameter; the run exits 1 at the M1 assertion.
//   HOLDS: M2, M5–M8, M10 and M11 are exact on both trees, and leak, panic and free-refusal
//   are clean on both — only the values of the cells with an allocation in the window move.
//
// M12–M14 carry their OWN falsification, against a different tree and a different channel:
// @falsified-at: a028b4f0e — interpret 1 stack-store free refusal(s) (BUG #306) -> 0; native
//   INERT (expected — the refusal is raised by the interpreter's allocator guard, which
//   `--native` has no counterpart for, so only one side can move)
//   CHANNEL: the FREE-REFUSAL channel, interpreter only.  Not the value channel — every cell
//   below prints the same string on the control, which is why this class went unguarded: the
//   control is WRONG and says so only on stderr, where nothing but `tests/wrap.rs` Part A2
//   reads it.
//   ARMED: none — unlike a leak, the refusal needs no instrument switched on: the allocator
//   raises it on every interpreter run and prints it to stderr.  What it DOES need is a reader.
//   A run scored on stdout and the exit code alone sees nothing at all, because the refusal
//   keeps the store alive and every assertion below still passes; `tests/wrap.rs` Part A2 is
//   what turns it into a failure, by snapshotting `loft::keys::stack_free_refusals()` around
//   the file.  So score this receipt through the corpus runner, or by reading stderr —
//   `loft --interpret <this file> 2>&1 | grep -c 'BUG (#306)'` — never by exit code.
//   WITNESS: on a028b4f0e `pick`'s three cells each raise `BUG (#306): refused to free the
//   stack store (#0)`; `ref_return`'s rename retired the `__retbuf` placeholder and left it an
//   owning local nothing declared, so the clear read an unreserved slot (loft#1651).
//   HOLDS: M1–M11 are exact on both trees, and leak, panic and assertions are clean on both.
// ⚠ The PATCH receipt beside this file belongs to the receipt ABOVE (M1–M11's value channel),
//   which is the stronger of the two — it moves on both backends.  Re-running `make falsify`
//   on this guard OVERWRITES that patch with whichever defect was last scored; it did here,
//   and the original was restored by hand.  Score M12–M14 with `REF=a028b4f0e` instead.
// ⚠ WHY M12–M14 EXIST — the gap that let a narrowing of the shared predicate land green.
// M6–M8 all call `same(w)`, whose EVERY return path hands back the parameter: the MUST-borrow
// shape.  The predicate those cells were used to justify also covers a second class, a callee
// ONE of whose paths borrows while another MINTS, and nothing here reached it.  Narrowing to
// "never free for a borrowing callee" therefore read as safe and took the source-free bit away
// from the bracket-licensed site too, where the minting arm then leaked one store per call —
// caught only by `1140-a-returned-keyed-parameter-is-still-the-callers.loft`, in another file
// and another spelling.  A guard that exercises only the trivial member of a class licenses a
// change to the whole class, so the mixed member is a cell here now.
//
// Which arm ran is a RUNTIME fact and no static bit can carry it (`formal/ownership.md` says
// so); the @P290 bracket is what decides it.  M14 makes that explicit by alternating the arms
// through ONE call site.  Each cell allocates a `spare` after the call so a wrongly-freed store
// has its slot recycled before the assertion reads it — without that churn a freed store
// answers correctly off stale bytes and the cell is vacuous.
//
// These cells could not land until loft#1651 was fixed: the RECORD spelling of this class
// raised a `BUG (#306)` stack-store free refusal that `tests/wrap.rs` Part A2 fails, in every
// destination, because `ref_return`'s rename retired the `__retbuf` placeholder and left it an
// owning local nothing declared.
struct V3 { x: single, y: single, z: single }
struct Vert { pos: V3, nrm: V3 }
struct Mesh { name: text, vs: vector<Vert> }
struct Hold { p: Vert, q: Vert }
fn v3(x: float, y: float, z: float) -> V3 { V3 { x: x as single, y: y as single, z: z as single } }
fn vert(p: V3, n: V3) -> Vert { Vert { pos: p, nrm: n } }
fn same(w: Vert) -> Vert { w }

fn show(m: Mesh) -> text {
  out = "";
  for i in 0..len(m.vs) { out += "{m.vs[i].pos.x},{m.vs[i].pos.y} "; }
  out
}

// M1 — the issue: expected 0,0 0,0 10,1 10,1 1,1 1,1 11,2 11,2
fn m1() -> text {
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [] };
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  for i in 0..2 {
    f = i as float;
    a = v3(f, f, -0.5);
    b = v3(f, f, 0.5);
    m.vs += [vert(a, up)];
    m.vs += [vert(b, up)];
    c = v3(f + 10.0, f + 1.0, -0.5);
    d = v3(f + 10.0, f + 1.0, 0.5);
    m.vs += [vert(c, up)];
    m.vs += [vert(d, up)];
  }
  show(m)
}

// M2 — straight line, a fresh call between two appends: 1,2 3,4 5,6
fn m2() -> text {
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [] };
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  a = v3(1.0, 2.0, 0.0);
  m.vs += [vert(a, up)];
  c = v3(3.0, 4.0, 0.0);
  m.vs += [vert(c, up)];
  e = v3(5.0, 6.0, 0.0);
  m.vs += [vert(e, up)];
  show(m)
}

// M3 — field assignment from a call, then an allocation: p 1,2 q 3,4 then r 7,8
fn m3() -> text {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  h = Hold { p: vert(up, up), q: vert(up, up) };
  for i in 0..2 {
    f = i as float;
    a = v3(1.0 + f, 2.0 + f, 0.0);
    h.p = vert(a, up);
    c = v3(3.0 + f, 4.0 + f, 0.0);
    h.q = vert(c, up);
  }
  r = v3(7.0, 8.0, 0.0);
  "{h.p.pos.x},{h.p.pos.y} {h.q.pos.x},{h.q.pos.y} {r.x},{r.y}"
}

// M4 — element assignment from a call in a loop, then an allocation: 11,12 13,14
fn m4() -> text {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [vert(up, up), vert(up, up)] };
  for i in 0..2 {
    f = i as float;
    a = v3(10.0 + f, 11.0 + f, 0.0);
    m.vs[0] = vert(a, up);
    c = v3(12.0 + f, 13.0 + f, 0.0);
    m.vs[1] = vert(c, up);
  }
  show(m)
}

// M5 — first assignment, the control: 1,2 3,4
fn m5() -> text {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  out = "";
  for i in 0..2 {
    f = i as float;
    x = vert(v3(1.0 + 2.0 * f, 2.0 + 2.0 * f, 0.0), up);
    c = v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0); if c.z > 100.0 { out += "!"; }
    out += "{x.pos.x},{x.pos.y} ";
  }
  out
}

// M6 — the callee hands back its argument: the caller's record survives: 4,5 4,5 | w 4,5
fn m6() -> text {
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [] };
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  w = vert(v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), up);
  for _ in 0..2 {
    m.vs += [same(w)];
    c = v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0); if c.z > 100.0 { m.name = "!"; }
  }
  "{show(m)}| w {w.pos.x},{w.pos.y}"
}

// M7 — field assignment from a callee that hands back its argument: w survives: 4,5 | w 4,5
fn m7() -> text {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  w = vert(v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), up);
  h = Hold { p: vert(up, up), q: vert(up, up) };
  for _ in 0..2 {
    h.q = same(w);
    c = v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0);
    h.p = vert(c, up);
  }
  "{h.q.pos.x},{h.q.pos.y} | w {w.pos.x},{w.pos.y}"
}

// M8 — element assignment from the same callee: 4,5 0,1 | w 4,5
fn m8() -> text {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  w = vert(v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), up);
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [vert(up, up), vert(up, up)] };
  for _ in 0..2 {
    m.vs[0] = same(w);
    c = v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0);
    m.vs[1] = vert(up, c);
  }
  "{show(m)}| w {w.pos.x},{w.pos.y}"
}

// M9 — the issue's own shape: the container is a PARAMETER, filled by a helper.
fn stair(m: Mesh, steps: integer) {
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  for i in 0..steps {
    f = i as float;
    a = v3(f, f, -0.5);
    b = v3(f, f, 0.5);
    m.vs += [vert(a, up)];
    m.vs += [vert(b, up)];
    c = v3(f + 10.0, f + 1.0, -0.5);
    d = v3(f + 10.0, f + 1.0, 0.5);
    m.vs += [vert(c, up)];
    m.vs += [vert(d, up)];
  }
}
fn m9() -> text {
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [] };
  stair(m, 2);
  show(m)
}

// M10 — the append under an `if` arm (even passes only): i=0 → 0,1  i=2 → 2,3, and a
// buffer minted after the arm on every pass: 0,1 2,3
fn m10() -> text {
  m = Mesh { name: "s", vs: [] };
  up = v3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  for i in 0..4 {
    f = i as float;
    if i % 2 == 0 { a = v3(f, f + 1.0, 0.0); m.vs += [vert(a, up)]; }
    c = v3(f + 50.0, 0.0, 0.0);
    if c.z > 100.0 { m.name = "!"; }
  }
  show(m)
}

// M11 — a keyed insert of a call result, then an allocation: keys 1, 2 hold 10, 20 → 10 20 2
struct Kv { k: integer, v: integer }
struct Table { rows: hash<Kv[k]> }
fn kv(k: integer, v: integer) -> Kv { Kv { k: k, v: v } }
fn m11() -> text {
  t = Table { rows: [] };
  for i in 1..3 {
    t.rows += [kv(i, i * 10)];
    c = v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0);
    if c.z > 100.0 { t.rows += [kv(99, 99)]; }
  }
  "{t.rows[1]?.v} {t.rows[2]?.v} {len(t.rows)}"
}

fn pick(w: Vert, c: boolean) -> Vert {
  fresh = Vert { pos: v3(9.0, 9.0, 9.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
  if c { w } else { fresh }
}

// M12 — the BORROW arm, three calls: the caller's `w` must survive every one.
fn m12() -> text {
  w = Vert { pos: v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
  o = "";
  for i in 0..3 {
    r = pick(w, true);
    spare = Vert { pos: v3(7.0, 7.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
    o += "{r.pos.x}/{w.pos.x}/{spare.pos.x} ";
  }
  o
}

// M13 — the MINT arm, three calls: the callee's store is its own to give away.  The VALUES
// are right whether or not it is released, so the leak channel is what scores this cell.
fn m13() -> text {
  w = Vert { pos: v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
  o = "";
  for i in 0..3 {
    r = pick(w, false);
    spare = Vert { pos: v3(7.0, 7.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
    o += "{r.pos.x}/{w.pos.x}/{spare.pos.x} ";
  }
  o
}

// M14 — the arms ALTERNATING through ONE call site: the same `OpCopyRecord` must free on the
// passes that minted and not on the passes that borrowed.
fn m14() -> text {
  w = Vert { pos: v3(4.0, 5.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
  o = "";
  for i in 0..4 {
    r = pick(w, i % 2 == 0);
    spare = Vert { pos: v3(7.0, 7.0, 0.0), nrm: v3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0) };
    o += "{r.pos.x}/{w.pos.x} ";
  }
  o
}

fn main() {
  r1 = m1(); r2 = m2(); r3 = m3(); r4 = m4(); r5 = m5(); r6 = m6(); r7 = m7(); r8 = m8(); r9 = m9(); r10 = m10(); r11 = m11();
  r12 = m12(); r13 = m13(); r14 = m14();
  println("M1 {r1}"); println("M2 {r2}"); println("M3 {r3}");
  println("M4 {r4}"); println("M5 {r5}"); println("M6 {r6}"); println("M7 {r7}"); println("M8 {r8}");
  println("M9 {r9}"); println("M10 {r10}"); println("M11 {r11}");
  assert(r1 == "0,0 0,0 10,1 10,1 1,1 1,1 11,2 11,2 ", "M1");
  assert(r2 == "1,2 3,4 5,6 ", "M2");
  assert(r3 == "2,3 4,5 7,8", "M3");
  assert(r4 == "11,12 13,14 ", "M4");
  assert(r5 == "1,2 3,4 ", "M5");
  assert(r6 == "4,5 4,5 | w 4,5", "M6");
  assert(r7 == "4,5 | w 4,5", "M7");
  assert(r8 == "4,5 0,1 | w 4,5", "M8");
  assert(r9 == "0,0 0,0 10,1 10,1 1,1 1,1 11,2 11,2 ", "M9");
  assert(r10 == "0,1 2,3 ", "M10");
  assert(r11 == "10 20 2", "M11");
  println("M12 {r12}"); println("M13 {r13}"); println("M14 {r14}");
  assert(r12 == "4/4/7 4/4/7 4/4/7 ", "M12");
  assert(r13 == "9/4/7 9/4/7 9/4/7 ", "M13");
  assert(r14 == "4/4 9/4 4/4 9/4 ", "M14");
}
